-- examples/checks/keyword-atom.rofl — `not` AS A WORD, NOT AS NEGATION.
--
-- The two hosts disagreed about this for as long as both have existed.
-- `p(not, bare).` loaded on the TypeScript side and was refused by the Rust
-- parser, whose `term` rejected a keyword wherever it appeared. Nothing in the
-- tree ever wrote the word down as data, so nothing said so — until a lint
-- recorded the words it found in comments as atoms, and a 33 406-row fact pack
-- was refused WHOLE by one engine while the other answered it.
--
-- The word is free in ARGUMENT POSITION, where there is no body element to
-- confuse it with, and reserved as a RELATION NAME, which is where the
-- ambiguity actually lives. That half is examples/checks/not-as-a-name.rofl.
--
-- The last clause is the probe worth having: one body carrying the word as an
-- argument AND as negation, a comma apart.

mention(a, not).
mention(b, and).
keyword_word(not).
quoted(b, "not").

flagged(X)  :- mention(X, W), keyword_word(W).
unquoted(X) :- mention(X, not), not quoted(X, "not").
